## Background

`parseJsonEventStream` in `@ai-sdk/provider-utils` buffers a server-sent
event until it is complete, and the buffer had no limit. A streaming
response that never completes an event, for example a `data:` line
without a newline, grew the buffer until the process ran out of memory.
The process then aborts with a V8 out-of-memory error, which an
application cannot catch.

Every provider that streams with `createEventSourceResponseHandler` is
affected, and so are the UI transports that read a UI message stream
with `parseJsonEventStream`. The other response handlers already have
limits: JSON responses since #16374 and JSON Lines rows since #22096.

## Summary

- `parseJsonEventStream` limits a single event to 64 \* 1024 \* 1024
characters, with the `maxBufferSize` option of `eventsource-parser`. The
limit covers a line that never ends and an event that never gets its
terminating blank line. A stream that exceeds the limit fails with the
parser's `ParseError` (`type: 'max-buffer-size-exceeded'`), and the
response body is cancelled.
- `eventsource-parser` is raised from `^3.0.8` to `^3.1.1`. The option
was added in 3.1.0, and the workspace was locked to 3.0.8, which ignores
it. 4.x is not used, because it is ESM-only and requires Node.js 22.12,
which the `release-v6.0` and `release-v5.0` backports cannot use. The
lockfile change is limited to the `packages/provider-utils` entry; 3.1.1
was already in the lockfile.
- `parseJsonEventStream` has a new optional `maxEventSize`. The gateway
video model uses it to accept a result event of up to 512 \* 1024 \*
1024 characters, because that event contains all generated videos, which
can be base64 encoded. That is about the maximum length of a string in
V8, so no result that could be parsed before is rejected now. This bound
is high, so a small heap can still be exhausted on this path; before
this change it had no limit.

This pull request also contains two CI changes, because the Node 22 and
Node 26 jobs failed without them:

- The Windows job no longer writes to the shared Turborepo remote cache
(`TURBO_CACHE: local:rw,remote:r`). It uploaded its `provider-utils`
build, and the Linux jobs restored it. A Windows checkout has CRLF line
endings, which end up in the source map of the build.
- The build staleness check in `response-handler.node.test.ts` ignores
line endings. It compares the sources in the source map with the files
on disk, so it failed for the build from Windows although the build was
current. It still fails for a stale build.

`packages/provider-utils/` changes because the buffering happens in the
SSE parsing that all providers share, so a provider-local fix would
leave the other providers unprotected. Well-formed streams behave as
before, unless a single event is larger than the limit.
`packages/gateway/` changes only to keep large video results working.
`packages/ai/` and `packages/provider/` are unchanged, but `useChat` and
`useCompletion` read their streams through `parseJsonEventStream`, so
the default limit applies to them too.

